    'intro_heading' => 'What is VirtuSphere?',
    'intro_p1' => 'VirtuSphere plans and deploys virtual machines for training and test missions. This web interface is the VirtuSphere portal, the operator-facing part of the web app; below, "portal" always refers to this interface (the machine-facing part of the web app is the machine API for MECM, Ansible and the Windows clients, with no interface of its own). A mission bundles a group of VMs that are created together on ESXi and configured through Ansible.',
    'intro_p2' => 'Templates are reusable mission blueprints (name starts with "_"). A template can be cloned into a new, independent mission at any time, with fresh VM IDs and empty MAC addresses.',

    'workflow_heading' => 'Workflow in short',
    'workflow_step1' => '1. Create a mission or template under "Missions" / "Templates" and define the VMs you need.',
    'workflow_step2' => '2. Add an ESXi credential and an Ansible credential under "Credentials" (if not already present).',
    'workflow_step3' => '3. Under "Deploy", pick the mission, ESXi and Ansible credential and the mode, then queue the job.',
    'workflow_step4' => '4. Follow progress live in the deploy log; MECM automatically picks up registered VMs for OS installation.',

    'roles_heading' => 'Administrator vs. User',
    'roles_p1' => 'There are exactly two roles. The role only controls which actions are visible and allowed in the portal. It has no effect on language or display.',
    'roles_matrix_th_feature' => 'Feature',
    'roles_matrix_view' => 'View the portal: dashboard, missions, VMs, packages, integrations, help',
    'perm_missions_write' => 'Create, edit and clone missions and templates',
    'perm_vms_write' => 'Create, edit and delete VMs (incl. network adapters and package assignment)',
    'perm_deploy_run' => 'Queue deploy jobs and view their logs',
    'perm_catalog_write' => 'Maintain VLANs (operating systems and packages come from MECM and are read-only)',
    'perm_credentials_manage' => 'Create and manage credentials (ESXi/Ansible)',
    'perm_system_config' => 'Change system settings (API base URL, IP allowlist, report channel token, protection thresholds, reachability probe)',
    'perm_users_manage' => 'Manage user accounts and view audit logs',

    'usersmgmt_heading' => 'User management: the "Users" page',
    'usersmgmt_p1' => 'The "Users" page is visible to administrators only. It lists every account with its role, active state, password-change requirement, current lockout and last sign-in. New accounts are created here and existing ones are managed here; there is deliberately no self-registration.',
    'usersmgmt_create_heading' => 'Creating a new account',
    'usersmgmt_create_p1' => 'A new account needs a name, an initial password (at least :min characters, per the current password policy) and a role; the email address is optional and serves as a note only. Hand the initial password to the person through a secure channel.',
    'usersmgmt_create_p2' => 'New accounts always start with a mandatory password change: on first sign-in the person must replace the initial password with their own. After the handover, nobody but the account holder knows the valid password.',
    'usersmgmt_actions_heading' => 'Managing existing accounts',
    'usersmgmt_action_role' => 'Change role: pick the new role in the account row and save. The change takes effect with the person\'s next page action; no re-login is required.',
    'usersmgmt_action_active' => 'Deactivate instead of delete: a deactivated account can no longer sign in but is kept with its history and can be reactivated at any time. Accounts are deliberately never deleted so audit entries stay attributable.',
    'usersmgmt_action_reset' => 'Reset password: enter a new password (at least :min characters) in the row field and reset. The mandatory change on next sign-in applies here as well.',
    'usersmgmt_safety_heading' => 'Built-in safeguards',
    'usersmgmt_safety_1' => 'You cannot deactivate your own account, so nobody locks themselves out by accident.',
    'usersmgmt_safety_2' => 'The last active administrator can neither be deactivated nor demoted to the "User" role. There is always at least one admin left.',
    'usersmgmt_safety_3' => 'After several failed sign-in attempts an account is locked automatically for :minutes minutes; the "Active" column then shows a "Locked" badge. The lock expires on its own; while a lock is active an administrator can also clear it immediately with "Clear lock".',
    'usersmgmt_safety_4' => 'One exception remains: you can demote your own role as long as another administrator exists. The portal asks first, because user management is closed to you afterwards; only the other administrator can undo it.',
    'usersmgmt_audit_p1' => 'Every account change (creation, role change, active state, password reset, own password change) is recorded in the audit log under "Security" with user and timestamp. Sign-ins, sign-outs, failed sign-in attempts and automatic account locks appear there as well.',

    'mission_heading' => 'Missions, templates and their link to MECM',
    'mission_p1' => 'A mission is a group of VMs that belong together and are deployed as one unit. Important to know: the mission name is created 1:1 as a device collection in SCCM, inside the "VirtuSphere_Missions" folder. Whatever is entered here in the portal shows up under exactly that name in the MECM console later.',
    'mission_p2' => 'Templates are recognisable by a name starting with "_". They are pure blueprint missions: they are never transferred to MECM and are the only place where VM names may repeat. A template can be cloned into a real mission at any time, with fresh VM IDs and empty MAC addresses.',
    'mission_p3' => 'The handover to MECM happens automatically: as soon as a VM has a MAC address (after deployment via ESXi), the sync script on the SCCM server imports it as a device and assigns it to its mission collection. Nobody has to create VMs in SCCM by hand.',

    'naming_heading' => 'Naming rules & data quality',
    'naming_p0' => 'Everything created in the portal has to work 1:1 in Windows and SCCM later. That leads to these rules:',
    'naming_p1' => 'Mission name: no whitespace. Once VMs of the mission have been transferred to MECM, the name is locked and can no longer be changed, because it is also the name of the SCCM collection, and renaming would break that link. If you want to rename, do it before the first deployment.',
    'naming_p2' => 'VM name: must be unique across all missions, because SCCM device names are global; two devices with the same name would interfere with each other in MECM. Only templates may repeat names; when cloning a template the portal lists all names that would collide up front.',
    'naming_p3' => 'Hostname: is the future Windows computer name (NetBIOS) and must follow its rules: at most 15 characters, only letters, digits and inner hyphens, no dots. Background: the client would silently shorten and clean the name during deploy, and the real name would then differ from what was entered. Older existing values that violate the rule stay valid and show a warning at the field; the rule only kicks in when the value is changed. Exception: grossly malformed legacy names (empty labels from double dots, a hyphen at the start or end of a label) are rejected even unchanged and must be corrected with the next save.',
    'naming_p4' => 'MAC addresses: the same address is written differently depending on the tool. With colons (00:50:56:AB:CD:EF, the way ESXi shows it), with hyphens (00-50-56-AB-CD-EF, the way Windows prints it in "ipconfig /all") or in Cisco dotted notation (0050.56ab.cdef). All three mean the same address. The portal always stores the colon form in upper case internally, no matter how the address was typed or imported. A hyphen in a MAC is therefore just a separator and makes no difference. That is why searches and comparisons work with any of these notations, and the device sync finds the VM in MECM regardless of how the MAC was originally entered. The automatic MAC import rejects duplicates (same MAC on another VM) and ambiguous cases (two NICs of the same VM in the same VLAN) with a clear error message instead of silently creating a wrong assignment.',

    'status_heading' => 'VM status: the five stages',
    'status_p1' => 'Every VM moves through five fixed stages during deployment. The stage shows how far the machine really is:',
    'status_1' => '1/5 Initializing: the VM only exists as a planning entry in the portal so far. Nothing has been created on ESXi yet.',
    'status_2' => '2/5 Registered: the VM has been created on ESXi and its MAC address is known. It is now waiting for the device sync to import it into MECM.',
    'status_3' => '3/5 Deployed: MECM has picked up the VM as a device and assigned it to the mission collection; the task sequence for the OS installation is starting.',
    'status_4' => '4/5 OS Installing: Windows is being installed right now. Detailed progress is shown in the "Client phases" section of the VM detail view.',
    'status_5' => '5/5 OS Installed: the installation is finished, the VM is ready for use.',
    'status_stuck_heading' => 'When a VM gets stuck',
    'status_stuck_1' => 'Stuck at 1/5: no deploy job has run yet. Go to "Deploy", pick the mission and queue a job.',
    'status_stuck_2' => 'Stuck at 2/5: first check whether the VM has a MAC address (VM detail view), because without a MAC the sync skips it. Important detail: the sync uses the MAC of the network interface in DHCP mode (the PXE interface); if no interface of the VM is set to DHCP, the VM is skipped as well. Then check the "Integrations" page: is "MECM device sync" at "OK"? And remember: templates are never transferred to MECM.',
    'status_stuck_3' => 'Stuck at 3/5 or 4/5: open the "Client phases" section in the VM detail view. It shows which installation step ran last and whether one of them failed.',

    'vm_delete_heading' => 'Decommissioning a VM',
    'vm_delete_p1' => 'The portal deliberately never deletes anything outside itself: neither the VM on the ESXi host nor the device in MECM. Both stay a conscious, manual decision. That is why it always takes three steps:',
    'vm_delete_step1' => '1. Power off and delete the VM on the ESXi host (host client or vCenter). The portal never does this by itself; if this step is forgotten, the machine keeps running there and keeps occupying datastore space.',
    'vm_delete_step2' => '2. Delete the VM in the portal. This automatically removes the database entry, the network interfaces, the package assignments and the status history.',
    'vm_delete_step3' => '3. Delete the device in the MECM console (Assets and Compliance → Devices). If a whole mission is dissolved, also remove its collection in the "VirtuSphere_Missions" folder.',
    'vm_delete_p2' => 'If step 3 is forgotten, nothing bad happens at first. But as soon as a new VM with the same name and a different MAC is created, the device sync reports a MAC conflict in the log and does not pick up the new VM automatically.',

    'deploy_heading' => 'Deploy modes',
    'deploy_requirements_p1' => 'A mission can only be queued once it has at least one VM and a datastore set; only the "Apply ESXi autostart policy" mode works without a datastore. Only one job runs per mission at a time; another is possible only after it finishes or is cancelled.',
    'deploy_p1' => 'Queuing a deploy job requires choosing one of these modes:',
    'deploy_mode_full' => 'Full pipeline: complete run. Create VMs, power-cycle, export MACs, start. When the mission enabled autostart, the autostart policy is written last.',
    'deploy_mode_autostart' => 'Apply ESXi autostart policy: writes the mission autostart policy to the host, without creating or starting VMs.',
    'deploy_mode_create' => 'Create VMs: creates the VMs on ESXi without starting them. Re-running it is not a way to apply changes to VMs that already exist; changed portal values only take effect when a VM is created anew.',
    'deploy_mode_powercycle' => 'Power-Cycle + Export MACs: briefly restarts VMs to read out MAC addresses.',
    'deploy_mode_export' => 'Export MACs: reads MAC addresses from VMs that are already running.',
    'deploy_mode_start' => 'Start VMs: starts VMs that already exist.',
    'deploy_verbose_heading' => 'Ansible -vvv',
    'deploy_verbose_p1' => 'The "Ansible -vvv" option raises Ansible output to triple verbosity (-vvv). The deploy log then additionally shows every single module action, the variables used and SSH connection details. This helps with troubleshooting but produces significantly more log lines and should stay off during normal operation.',
    'deploy_powercycle_wait_heading' => 'Power-cycle wait',
    'deploy_powercycle_wait_p1' => 'The wait time controls how long to wait between powering off and on during a power-cycle. It applies only to the "Full pipeline" and "Power-Cycle + Export MACs" modes; in every other mode the form disables the field because no power-cycle happens.',

    'credentials_heading' => 'Credentials',
    'credentials_p1' => 'ESXi and Ansible credentials are stored encrypted and are offered as a choice when queuing a deploy job. Without at least one ESXi and one Ansible credential, plus a reachable API base URL, no job can be queued.',

    'packages_heading' => 'Packages & versions',
    'packages_p1' => 'Packages are not created in the portal but on the SCCM server. There the autoimporter reads every package folder with its config.json (required fields ProjectName and version) and builds an SCCM application including a device collection. Its name is composed from both fields: ProjectName, a hyphen, then the version, for example "Firefox-115.0". The package sync reports exactly these collection names via HTTP to the portal, which only displays the catalog and links packages to VMs.',
    'packages_p1b' => 'The portal reverses this composition to show base name and version separately and to keep multiple versions of the same package distinguishable. It splits at the last hyphen: everything before it is the base name, everything after it is the version. This is necessary because the base name itself may contain hyphens (such as "Adobe-Reader-DC") while the version may not. Only under this rule is the last hyphen unambiguously the boundary between name and version. For package maintenance on the SCCM server this means: in config.json the version must not contain a hyphen, while the ProjectName may.',
    'packages_p2' => 'If a package disappears from MECM it is not deleted but marked as "Retired"; existing VM assignments are kept. If an active successor version exists, assignments are relinked to it automatically. Retired packages without any assignment are removed for good after :days days.',
    'packages_p3' => 'The package list can be filtered by status (Active/Retired). The VM editor hides retired packages; if a VM is still linked to a retired version it stays visible and a hint shows the newer version that is available.',

    'packages_os_heading' => 'Operating systems',
    'packages_os_p1' => 'Like packages, operating systems are not maintained in the portal; they come from MECM. The same package sync also reports every Task Sequence on the SCCM server, and from these the operating system catalog is built, which the "Operating Systems" page displays. An entry is only effective when its name matches a Task Sequence exactly, because the operating system assigned to a VM selects that Task Sequence for the PXE boot. That is why entries cannot be created or edited here: a hand-typed name without a matching Task Sequence would point nowhere at PXE boot.',
    'packages_os_p2' => 'New Task Sequences appear automatically on the next sync (default cadence 60 seconds, plus a full reconciliation every hour). When a Task Sequence is deleted in MECM, the entry does not disappear but becomes "Retired"; assigned VMs keep their choice, and the entry is only removed after :days days once no VM still uses it. An administrator can also delete an entry by hand: if the Task Sequence still exists in MECM, the next sync re-creates it (self-healing); if the sync is down (MECM outage), it stays missing until it returns, without harming existing VMs, whose assignment uses the stored name. If all Task Sequences are removed or MECM currently returns none, the catalog stays unchanged instead of being emptied.',

    'logs_heading' => 'Logs & troubleshooting',
    'logs_p1' => 'There are two kinds of logs. The deploy log (the "Log" link on each job under "Deploy") shows the output of the running Ansible job in real time; this is where to look when a deployment fails. The audit log under "Logs" (administrators only) records who changed what in the system and when.',
    'logs_p2' => 'The audit log is split into four sections (tabs at the top) so it is not one undifferentiated table: "Security" (sign-in & access, users, credentials), "Resources" (missions, VMs, VLANs, operating systems), "Deployment" (deploy, MECM integration) and "System & integrations" (system, settings, legacy API). Within a section you can further full-text search and filter by IP and by an individual category. The "MECM integration" category deliberately contains only significant events: a monitored source going down or coming back, automatically relinked package assignments and rejected sync runs. Individual heartbeats are not logged, otherwise the log would become unreadable within hours.',
    'logs_p2b' => 'The "Security" section records who has and had access to the system: successful and failed sign-ins, automatic account and IP locks, sign-outs, expired sessions, password changes, and refused requests (missing permission or invalid CSRF token). Change entries name the changed fields with their old and new value; secrets and notes only show as "changed". Data egress is recorded too: the JSON export of a mission and the CSV list exports. Deploy jobs log their outcome (succeeded or failed with the reason) in addition to being queued.',
    'logs_p3' => 'Cleanup is automatic via the maintenance service: security logs (sign-in, users, credentials) are kept for :security_days days, all other portal log entries for :other_days days, the sign-in attempt counter for :attempt_days days, client phase reports for :client_days days, retired packages without VM assignments for :package_days days. Nobody has to delete logs by hand.',
    'logs_p4' => 'The output of deploy jobs is cleaned up too, but only :days days after the job has finished. A running job never loses a line. The job itself stays in the list with its status and error message; opening its log later shows a note instead of an empty table. The mission-less system jobs of the ESXi inventory pulls are removed entirely on the same window: no list shows them, and their lasting result is the traffic light on the integrations page.',

    'stack_heading' => 'The whole stack in questions & answers',
    'stack_p1' => 'How MECM, database, web app, PowerShell, Ansible, ESXi and the machine API work together: from the web interface to the finished Windows VM. Click a question to expand it.',
    'stack_q1' => 'What are the parts of the system?',
    'stack_a1_p1' => 'VirtuSphere is not a single program but an interplay of several building blocks. Each one has a clearly bounded job, and none knows the internals of the others; they only exchange data over a few fixed interfaces:',
    'stack_a1_li1' => 'The portal: this web interface, a PHP application. Planning, monitoring and administration happen here; heavy, long-running work is deliberately not executed by it.',
    'stack_a1_li2' => 'The MySQL database: the memory of the system. Missions, VMs, network interfaces, jobs, credentials and logs live here.',
    'stack_a1_li3' => 'The deploy worker: a background service that processes the job queue and triggers the Ansible runs.',
    'stack_a1_li4' => 'The maintenance service: a second background service for cleanup jobs, the MECM reachability check and its own heartbeat.',
    'stack_a1_li5' => 'Ansible on a Linux host reachable via SSH: executes the actual ESXi commands (create, power, read out VMs).',
    'stack_a1_li6' => 'The ESXi server: the virtualisation platform where the VMs actually come to life.',
    'stack_a1_li7' => 'The MECM/SCCM server with three PowerShell scripts plus the Windows clients themselves: they install Windows and report progress back.',
    'stack_a1_p2' => 'Portal, database and the two background services run together as Docker containers on an Ubuntu host; the next question explains this runtime environment in more detail.',

    'stack_q2' => 'What does the web app itself run on? (Ubuntu and Docker)',
    'stack_a2_p1' => 'The entire web app runs on a single Ubuntu server on the LAN, fully inside Docker containers. Docker wraps each service in its own isolated environment with a pinned version; apart from Docker itself, nothing needs to be installed on the Ubuntu host. Everything is started and managed together via Docker Compose (the "docker compose" command in the project directory). These containers belong to the stack:',
    'stack_a2_li1' => 'webserver: nginx, accepts all HTTP requests from the LAN (port 8021) and hands PHP pages to the PHP container.',
    'stack_a2_li2' => 'php: runs the PHP code of the portal and the machine API (PHP 8.4).',
    'stack_a2_li3' => 'mysql: the database (MySQL 8.4); its data lives permanently in a data directory on the host and survives every container restart.',
    'stack_a2_li4' => 'deploy-worker and maintenance-worker: the two background services for deployments and maintenance.',
    'stack_a2_li5' => 'phpmyadmin: a database administration interface for administrators.',
    'stack_a2_p2' => 'All containers carry the restart rule "unless-stopped": after a crash or a reboot of the Ubuntu host they come back automatically without anyone stepping in. The system is deliberately built LAN-only: no internet access required, no external services, no telemetry. Communication starts out over HTTP; the switch to HTTPS is planned as a later administrative step.',

    'stack_q3' => 'Where is which data stored, and what is the "truth"?',
    'stack_a3_p1' => 'The web app\'s MySQL database is the memory for the desired state: missions, VMs, network interfaces, jobs, credentials and logs. Credentials (ESXi, Ansible) are never stored in plaintext but encrypted in the database. "Desired state" means: the portal describes what the VMs are supposed to look like. On ESXi a change only takes effect when the VM is created there; a VM that already exists is not reconfigured.',
    'stack_a3_p2' => 'MECM is the truth for the end of a device\'s life: deletion there is deliberately manual only, so that no automatism accidentally removes productive devices. Package definitions live as config.json folders on the SCCM server, operating systems as Task Sequences; both catalogs are only displayed in the portal, not created or edited there.',
    'stack_a3_p3' => 'For the virtualization layer, ESXi itself is the truth: datacenters, datastores and port groups are maintained there. The portal reads these values read-only at fixed intervals and keeps only a mirror of them, the ESXi inventory. The mirror displays and warns about deviations but never blocks a deployment; changes are always made on the ESXi host, never in the portal.',
    'stack_a3_p4' => 'This split is intentional: every piece of information has exactly one place where it is changed. To create or change a VM, use the portal; to change a package, the SCCM server; to dispose of a device, the MECM console; to change a datastore or port group, the ESXi host. Every other place only shows a mirror of it, which is exactly why they cannot be maintained contradictorily.',

    'stack_q4' => 'What happens when I queue a deploy job?',
    'stack_a4_p1' => 'The portal only writes the job into the database queue; it does not execute anything itself. There is a practical reason: an Ansible run can take many minutes, and a web page should not block that long. The actual work is done by the deploy worker, a dedicated container that continuously watches the queue:',
    'stack_a4_li1' => 'The deploy worker picks up the next waiting job from the queue.',
    'stack_a4_li2' => 'It generates the Ansible files (inventory and variables) with all VM data of the mission.',
    'stack_a4_li3' => 'It uploads the files via SFTP to the Ansible host and starts the playbook there via SSH.',
    'stack_a4_li4' => 'Ansible talks to the ESXi server and creates the VMs, powers them on or off or reads them out, depending on the chosen mode.',
    'stack_a4_li5' => 'The run output streams back live into the deploy log; at the end the job is marked as succeeded or failed.',
    'stack_a4_p2' => 'Further jobs simply wait in the queue meanwhile. Processing follows the order of queueing, with two exceptions: a job with a schedule is only picked up once its time has come, and mission deploys take their turn before the mission-less system jobs of the ESXi inventory fetches; a burst of automatic fetches therefore never delays a deploy. If the deploy worker is down, nothing is lost: the jobs stay in the queue and continue after its restart.',

    'stack_q5' => 'How do the VMs\' MAC addresses get into the portal?',
    'stack_a5_p1' => 'ESXi only assigns MAC addresses once the VM has been created. Before that, the VM exists in the portal only as a planning entry without a MAC, which is exactly why MECM cannot know it at that point.',
    'stack_a5_p2' => 'In the power-cycle step the VMs are briefly powered on and off again; the playbook then reads the MACs from ESXi and sends them via HTTP to the web app\'s machine API. There they are strictly validated before being stored: the same MAC must not already belong to another VM, and the assignment to the right network interface must be unambiguous; two interfaces of the same VM in the same VLAN would be ambiguous and are rejected with a clear error message.',
    'stack_a5_p3' => 'Only with a known MAC can MECM reliably recognise the VM later, because during PXE boot the MAC is the only attribute the device can identify itself with. If the upload fails (wrong API base URL, missing IP allowlist entry), the VMs get stuck without a MAC and never reach the MECM handover; this is the most common stumbling block when setting up a new environment.',

    'stack_q6' => 'How does MECM learn about new VMs?',
    'stack_a6_p1' => 'The SCCM server fetches them itself: the "device sync" PowerShell script (scheduled task) asks the web app API every 10 seconds for VMs with a MAC address. The web app never accesses SCCM itself (apart from the plain reachability check); the scripts always come to it. This pull principle simplifies firewall and permissions: the SCCM server only needs HTTP access to the web app, nothing else.',
    'stack_a6_p2' => 'The script imports new VMs as devices into SCCM (recognised by MAC), approves them and assigns them via direct membership to their collections: the collection of their operating system (folder VirtuSphere_OS), the collections of their assigned packages and the collection of their mission (folder VirtuSphere_Missions). Missing collections and folders are created by the script itself.',
    'stack_a6_p3' => 'Finally it reports the SCCM device ID (ResourceID) back to the web app; with that the VM moves to stage 3/5 in the portal. Conflicts are handled deliberately cautiously: if the MAC of an existing SCCM device does not match the one from the portal, only a warning is logged and nothing is overwritten automatically.',

    'stack_q7' => 'How does Windows get onto the VM?',
    'stack_a7_p1' => 'Via PXE network boot: the VM boots from its network interface in the WDS VLAN, the only VLAN where a PXE server answers. MECM recognises the device by its MAC and starts the task sequence of its operating system collection, which installs Windows fully automatically; which task sequence that is, is determined solely by the operating system chosen for the VM in the portal.',
    'stack_a7_p2' => 'After the Windows base installation the assigned packages take over: in SCCM they are bound to their package collections as required deployments, and since the device sync has put the VM into exactly these collections, Software Center installs them without further intervention.',
    'stack_a7_p3' => 'In parallel the four client phases run (getinfo, hostname, staticip, disks): small scripts that in order report device info, rename the computer, switch the network to the target configuration and activate additional disks. Every phase reports its progress to the web app, visible in the "Client phases" section of the VM detail view. With the last report the VM reaches stage 5/5 and is ready for use.',

    'stack_q8' => 'Where does the package list in the portal come from?',
    'stack_a8_p1' => 'From MECM, fully automatically in two steps. First: the autoimporter on the SCCM server watches a package folder there. To provide a new package, drop a folder with the installation files and a small config.json (name, version, options); the script autonomously builds the SCCM application including collection and deployment in the console folder VirtuSphere_Applications.',
    'stack_a8_p2' => 'Second: the package sync regularly reports the contents of exactly this folder via HTTP to the web app. The name is split at the last hyphen into base name and version ("Firefox-115.0" becomes Firefox in version 115.0; the base name may itself contain hyphens, the version may not). If a package disappears from MECM it is not deleted in the portal but marked as "Retired"; a configurable protection threshold additionally prevents a faulty run from accidentally retiring large parts of the catalog at once.',
    'stack_a8_p3' => 'The portal only displays the catalog and links packages to VMs; creating, changing or deleting packages happens exclusively on the SCCM server. The same holds for operating systems: they come as Task Sequences via the same sync and are only displayed in the portal, not maintained. That way there is exactly one place of maintenance and no two competing catalogs that could drift apart.',

    'stack_q9' => 'Who connects to whom, and in which direction?',
    'stack_a9_p1' => 'Almost everything comes to the web app, not the other way round. The connections at a glance:',
    'stack_a9_li1' => 'Browser to web app (HTTP): humans operate the portal.',
    'stack_a9_li2' => 'SCCM scripts to web app (HTTP): fetch VM lists, report device IDs, package catalog and heartbeats.',
    'stack_a9_li3' => 'Windows clients to web app (HTTP): report client phases. These are PowerShell scripts as well; they are distributed to the freshly installed VMs via the MECM Software Center and run locally there.',
    'stack_a9_li4' => 'Ansible host to web app (HTTP): upload the MAC addresses read from ESXi.',
    'stack_a9_li5' => 'Web app to Ansible host (SSH/SFTP): upload deployment files and start playbooks.',
    'stack_a9_li6' => 'Web app to SCCM server (TCP): plain reachability check for the status row under "Integrations".',
    'stack_a9_li7' => 'Ansible host to ESXi (API): create, power and read out VMs.',
    'stack_a9_p2' => 'These directions are deliberate: no external system needs credentials to the web app, the firewall only has to allow a few paths, and a compromised client could at most fake its own reports. Incoming machine access is additionally protected by IP allowlisting and an optional token.',

    'stack_q10' => 'What does "API" refer to?',
    'stack_a10_p1' => 'Besides the portal pages for humans, the web app has a machine interface: individual HTTP endpoints through which the SCCM scripts fetch VM lists and report device IDs and package catalogs, Ansible delivers the MAC addresses, and the Windows clients report their installation phases.',
    'stack_a10_p2' => 'It is protected in several layers: it is only reachable on the LAN; server access (SCCM, Ansible) must be on the IP allowlist (portal, "Settings" page); the server heartbeats can additionally require a shared token; and Windows clients identify themselves via a MAC address the web app already knows, so they need no entry of their own and no token.',
    'stack_a10_p3' => 'There is no web interface for the machine API; what happens there shows up indirectly on the "Integrations" (heartbeats) and "Logs" (important events) pages. Nobody needs to know the exact endpoints and data formats for daily operations; they do not change without a deliberate migration decision, because the scripts are built firmly on top of them.',

    'stack_q11' => 'What happens when a component fails?',
    'stack_a11_p1' => 'The system is built for self-healing; nothing breaks permanently. The typical cases:',
    'stack_a11_li1' => 'SCCM server or one of its scripts fails: new VMs stay at stage 2/5, the source under "Integrations" reports "Down". After a restart the sync catches up on everything; it even recreates required console folders when needed.',
    'stack_a11_li2' => 'Web app briefly unreachable: the SCCM scripts and Windows clients simply keep retrying with backoff; reports are not lost, they just arrive later.',
    'stack_a11_li3' => 'Deploy worker down: jobs stay in the queue and continue after it starts.',
    'stack_a11_li4' => 'Ansible host unreachable: a running job fails visibly with its full output in the deployment log; once the host is back, simply queue it again. The ESXi inventory fetch also runs through this host and then reports the "Ansible host" category.',
    'stack_a11_li5' => 'ESXi unreachable or login wrong: the inventory fetch fails and the traffic light changes colour, but the last known data is kept; nothing is emptied. On a login failure the automatic fetch pauses until the credential is changed, so the ESXi account does not get locked.',
    'stack_a11_li6' => 'Ubuntu host reboots: all containers come back automatically through the restart rule; the scheduled tasks on the SCCM server also start at system startup.',
    'stack_a11_p2' => 'Only lost data cannot be replaced by self-healing; that is what the backup is for (next question).',

    'stack_q12' => 'How is the system backed up, and how do you restore?',
    'stack_a12_p1' => 'On the Ubuntu host via script: "sh scripts/backup.sh" writes two files per run to Docker/backups/: a complete, compressed database dump and an archive of the configuration (.env, docker-compose.yml, nginx configuration). The script verifies the dump\'s integrity and keeps the newest runs of both files (14 by default). A daily cron job plus a copy to a second host is recommended, because the backups contain secrets and belong in an access-restricted place.',
    'stack_a12_p2' => 'Whether a backup really works is only proven by a restore: "sh scripts/restore_test.sh" loads the latest dump into a throwaway container and checks the tables without touching the running stack. The package definitions (config.json folders) live on the SCCM server and must be included in its own backup there. The complete guide including disaster restore is in the operations handbook docs/operations/backup.md.',

    'firstaid_heading' => 'First steps when something fails',
    'firstaid_p1' => 'Whatever is stuck, the order is always the same. This is how to find out within minutes which component is affected:',
    'firstaid_step1' => 'Open the "Integrations" page: is any source at "Delayed" or "Down"? If so, the matching action hint is shown right next to it; the typical diagnoses further down on this page help as well.',
    'firstaid_step2' => 'If a deployment failed: under "Deploy", click "Log" on the affected job. It contains the complete Ansible output with the actual error message.',
    'firstaid_step3' => 'If a VM is stuck in the Windows installation (stage 3/5 or 4/5): open the "Client phases" section in the VM detail view. It shows which step ran last and whether one of them failed.',
    'firstaid_step4' => 'If that is not enough: the operations handbook in the project folder (docs/operations/mecm-integration.md) describes the log files on the SCCM server and the clients, plus the most common cases step by step.',

    'integrations_heading' => 'The "Integrations" page: health check at a glance',
    'integrations_p1' => 'VirtuSphere only works together with scripts running on the SCCM server. The "Integrations" page is the first place to look when something does not arrive: it shows one status row per component, with the last report and the check interval. If a row is at "Down", a plain-language action hint is shown right next to it.',
    'integrations_p2' => 'The page is deliberately visible to every signed-in user, not just administrators. The first diagnosis therefore needs no admin rights: anyone can check whether a source is at "Down" before reporting a problem.',
    'integrations_sources_heading' => 'What is monitored?',
    'integrations_source_1' => 'MECM device sync: script on the SCCM server. Imports new VMs as devices into SCCM and assigns them to their mission collections. Without this script every VM stays stuck at stage 2/5.',
    'integrations_source_2' => 'MECM package sync: script on the SCCM server. Reports the SCCM application catalog and the Task Sequences to the portal; this is where the "Packages" and "Operating Systems" pages come from.',
    'integrations_source_3' => 'MECM package import (autoimporter): script on the SCCM server. Automatically builds SCCM applications including collection and deployment from dropped package folders (config.json).',
    'integrations_source_4' => 'MECM server reachable: active connection check. The web app itself tests every :minutes minutes whether the SCCM server responds on the network. This distinguishes "server is gone" from "only the scripts stopped".',
    'integrations_source_5' => 'Maintenance service (web app): internal background service of the web app. Runs the reachability check and the cleanup jobs and reports itself every minute.',
    'integrations_status_heading' => 'Reading the status display',
    'integrations_status_p1' => 'Every source reports on its own at a fixed pace ("heartbeat"). When a report stops arriving, the status changes. These are the same coloured status marks as on the "Integrations" page:',
    'integrations_work_heading' => 'Working with the status hints',
    'integrations_work_1' => 'On "Delayed" or "Down", first read the hint text right next to the row. It states the most likely cause and the next step in plain language; nobody has to memorise what each source means.',
    'integrations_work_2' => 'Carry out the step or hand it over precisely: some measures (starting scheduled tasks on the SCCM server, changing settings) need admin or server access. In that case report the affected row together with its hint text to the responsible person; that saves them half the diagnosis.',
    'integrations_work_3' => 'After the fix, wait briefly: every source reports on its fixed schedule (column "Interval"). The row only returns to "OK" with the next heartbeat, not immediately after the intervention.',
    'integrations_work_4' => 'If the row stays at "Down" anyway: continue with the typical diagnoses below and then consult the operations handbook (docs/operations/mecm-integration.md).',
    'integrations_fix_heading' => 'Typical diagnoses',
    'integrations_fix_1' => 'Sync sources at "Down", but "MECM server reachable" at "OK": the server is running, only the scheduled tasks are not reporting. Open Task Scheduler on the SCCM server and start the tasks "VirtuSphere MECM Devices Sync", "VirtuSphere MECM Packages Sync" or "VirtuSphere MECM Package Import".',
    'integrations_fix_2' => '"MECM server reachable" at "Down": the server itself cannot be reached. Is it powered on? Are network and firewall between the web app host and the SCCM server okay? Administrators can adjust the check target address and port under Settings.',
    'integrations_fix_3' => '"Maintenance service" at "Down": the internal web app service has stopped. On the web app host (Ubuntu), check "docker compose ps" in the project directory and restart with "docker compose up -d maintenance-worker".',
    'integrations_fix_4' => 'For deeper cases (log files on the SCCM server, script edge cases) there is an operations handbook in the project folder: docs/operations/mecm-integration.md.',

    'mecmfolders_heading' => 'Folder structure in the MECM console: what lives where?',
    'mecmfolders_p1' => 'Everything VirtuSphere creates lives in three fixed folders in the MECM console. This keeps it cleanly separated from manually maintained SCCM objects, and new staff can find their way around immediately:',
    'mecmfolders_item1' => 'VirtuSphere_OS (under Assets and Compliance → Device Collections): one collection per task sequence, with the same name. VMs receive their operating system through these; created by the device sync.',
    'mecmfolders_item2' => 'VirtuSphere_Missions (also under Device Collections): one collection per mission, named exactly like the mission in the portal; created by the device sync.',
    'mecmfolders_item3' => 'VirtuSphere_Applications: exists twice, once under Device Collections (one collection "Name-Version" per package) and once under Software Library → Applications (the applications themselves); populated by the autoimporter. Important: the package sync reads exactly this collections folder as its catalog source. Only collections inside this folder appear as packages in the portal.',
    'mecmfolders_p2' => 'All three folders are created by the scripts themselves when needed, nobody has to create them by hand (requires the ConfigMgr console version 2111 or later; on older versions create the folders manually once). The devices themselves land in "All Systems" without a dedicated folder and are only assigned to the collections.',
    'mecmfolders_p3' => 'For day-to-day work this means: a package whose collection sits outside VirtuSphere_Applications does not show up in the portal; after moving it into the folder it does. And please do not rename the folders or their contents, because the names are the link between the portal and SCCM.',

    'clientphases_heading' => 'Client phases: watching the Windows installation',
    'clientphases_p1' => 'From stage 3/5 on it is no longer the portal doing the work but the VM itself: small scripts on the freshly installed Windows report their progress back. The "Client phases" section in the VM detail view shows four steps that always run in this order:',
    'clientphases_step1' => 'getinfo: the client fetches its device information (including network interfaces) from the web app by MAC and stores it locally in the registry; the following phases (hostname, staticip) read their target values from there. This is why getinfo must always run first.',
    'clientphases_step2' => 'hostname: the computer is renamed to the hostname stored in the portal and then reboots.',
    'clientphases_step3' => 'staticip: the network is switched to the target configuration; the VM often changes VLAN in the process and is briefly unreachable.',
    'clientphases_step4' => 'disks: additional disks are brought online (optional; VMs without extra disks skip this step).',
    'clientphases_p2' => 'Every phase reports "started", "finished" or "failed". The status "Executed, confirmation pending" appears when the start was reported but the completion report has been missing for :minutes minutes. This is usually harmless: after the rename the VM reboots, after the network switch it sits in a different VLAN; in both cases the completion report simply cannot reach the web app any more.',
    'clientphases_p3' => 'Only "Failed" with a detail text is a real error. In that case the script log directly on the affected VM under C:\\Program Files\\aplw\\Logs helps.',

    'settings_heading' => 'Settings: the admin area step by step',
    'settings_p1' => 'The "Settings" page is visible to administrators only and bundles every system-wide switch. It is organized into four areas: Deployment, Machine API, Catalog and inventory, and System. Each change takes effect immediately (no restart needed) and is recorded in the audit log under "System & integrations → Settings" with user and time. For rotating staff: the defaults are deliberately safe. When in doubt, change nothing and first check the "Deploy runtime" card in the Deployment area.',

    'settings_api_heading' => 'API base URL (deploy)',
    'settings_api_p1' => 'This is the address at which the web app is reachable on the LAN. The deploy worker writes it into every generated Ansible file; Ansible uses it to upload the MAC addresses it reads out, and all status reports flow back to it. If the value is wrong or unreachable, VMs are still created on ESXi but stay stuck at stage 2/5 without a MAC address.',
    'settings_api_p2' => 'Rule of thumb: if Docker and Ansible run on the same Ubuntu host, http://127.0.0.1:8021 usually fits. If Ansible comes from a different machine, enter the host or DNS address reachable from there, e.g. http://virtusphere.lan:8021. What matters is whether the test on the Ansible host works: "curl http://<address>/portal/health.php". Without a valid, reachable API base URL no deploy job can be queued.',

    'settings_allowlist_heading' => 'Machine API IP allowlist',
    'settings_allowlist_p1' => 'This list decides which machines may call the machine API: the SCCM server (device sync, package sync, reporting the device ID) and the Ansible host (MAC upload). If an IP is not on the list it gets "Access denied" (403). An empty list therefore rejects all script and Ansible access, and the sync sources on "Integrations" stay at "Down".',
    'settings_allowlist_p2' => 'Enter the IP addresses of the SCCM server and the Ansible host, each with a short description so the next person knows what an entry is for. Windows clients need no entry here: they identify themselves via a MAC address the web app already knows. If a server\'s IP changes, delete the old entry here and add the new one.',

    'settings_token_heading' => 'Report channel token (optional)',
    'settings_token_p1' => 'An optional shared secret the SCCM server sync tasks use to identify themselves to the report channel (mecm_report.php), on top of the IP allowlist. It applies to the server heartbeats only. How it works: the sync tasks send it in the HTTP header "X-VirtuSphere-Token" with every heartbeat; the web app stores only its SHA-256 hash and compares against that, so the plaintext never sits in the database. Only heartbeats with a matching token are accepted. The client-phase reports from the deployed VMs deliberately do not need the token: they identify themselves via their already-known MAC, so the token never has to be distributed to every ephemeral VM. What for and why: the token makes sure the heartbeats really come from the genuine SCCM server, not some arbitrary host on the LAN; forged heartbeats could otherwise mislead the traffic lights on the "Integrations" page (the channel never changes deploy or status decisions itself, display only).',
    'settings_token_p2' => 'On generation the token is shown in plaintext exactly once: copy it immediately, it cannot be shown again later (only the hash is stored). On the MECM server it belongs in the registry under HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\VirtuSphere\\MECM, value "ReportToken". The easiest way is to re-run the installer install-VirtuSphere-MECM.ps1: it prompts for the token without echoing it and writes it into the admin-only readable registry. Do not pass it as a plaintext parameter, otherwise it ends up in the PowerShell history.',
    'settings_token_p3' => 'If no token is set, the report channel stays protected by the IP allowlist alone; that is allowed. Once a token is set the button reads "Regenerate token" and asks first: it replaces the existing token, which stops working immediately. Enter the new value on the MECM server afterwards, otherwise its reports are rejected. "Clear token" turns the token requirement off again.',

    'settings_retire_heading' => 'Package sync protection threshold',
    'settings_retire_p1' => 'The package sync reconciles the portal catalog with MECM automatically; packages that no longer appear there are retired. Retiring is not deleting: the package only drops out of the selection, and existing VM assignments stay intact. This threshold is a brake against accidental mass retirement: if a single sync would retire more than the configured percentage of active packages at once, the whole run is rejected instead of executed. That way a single fault, such as a partially read catalog or a wrong folder on the SCCM server, does not take down the whole catalog. The default is :default %, adjustable between :min and :max %.',
    'settings_retire_p2' => 'Rejected runs appear in the log under "MECM integration"; check the catalog source first, since it is usually a WMI hiccup or a wrong collections folder. If a legitimate large cleanup is due where many packages are removed on purpose, raise the value temporarily and set it back afterwards.',

    'settings_esxi_heading' => 'ESXi inventory fetch',
    'settings_esxi_p1' => 'Sets how often the portal reads datacenters, datastores, portgroups and host capacity read-only from the registered ESXi credentials; the results appear on the "Integrations" page. The interval is in hours (0 turns the automatic fetch off), up to :max hours are allowed. The fetch uses the existing Ansible credential; if there are several, one must be chosen or it pauses.',
    'settings_esxi_p2_interval_zero' => 'With interval 0 the integrations traffic light no longer warns about the age of the last fetch: the automation is deliberately off, so age proves nothing. Failed fetches and paused credentials are still shown.',

    'settings_probe_heading' => 'MECM reachability probe',
    'settings_probe_p1' => 'Controls the "MECM server reachable" status row on the "Integrations" page. The maintenance service opens a TCP connection to the given host and port every :minutes minutes. This distinguishes whether the server is gone entirely or only the scheduled tasks on it have stopped.',
    'settings_probe_p2' => 'Leaving the host empty means "automatic": the source IP of the last device-sync heartbeat is probed. An entry here overrides that (e.g. sccm-server.lan or 10.0.0.10). The default port is 445 (SMB); only change it if a different port on the SCCM server answers reliably in your network.',

    'settings_runtime_heading' => 'Runtime info (display only)',
    'settings_runtime_p1' => 'The "Deploy runtime" card in the Deployment area cannot be edited. For verification it shows the actually effective API URL (after resolving the setting) and the Ansible mode (SSH/SFTP to the Ubuntu host). If a hint appears there instead of the URL, the API base URL above is not set yet or is faulty; fix that first.',

    'settings_session_heading' => 'Session lifetime',
    'settings_session_p1' => 'The session lifetime is an absolute deadline, not an idle timer: when it runs out the sign-in ends, no matter how much was clicked in between. Ordinary navigation deliberately does not extend it.',
    'settings_session_p2' => 'A dialog appears :warn minutes before expiry. Only explicitly extending in that dialog resets the deadline; whoever does not react is signed out and will not find their input in the form any more. Short lifetimes are safer but interrupt long forms such as VM editing more often.',
    'settings_session_p3' => 'Allowed are :min to :max minutes (:default by default). A change applies to new sign-ins and to the next extension; sessions already running keep their remaining time.',

    'settings_password_heading' => 'Password policy',
    'settings_password_p1' => 'The minimum length applies to every newly set password: your own change in the account page, creating an account, and an admin reset. Portal and server check the same rule, there is no way around the form.',
    'settings_password_p2' => 'Allowed are :min to :max characters (:default by default); :min is the floor and cannot be undercut. After an increase, existing passwords stay valid until they are next changed. To enforce the new length right away, reset the accounts in question in the user administration; the new length then applies together with the mandatory change on next sign-in.',

    'settings_backup_heading' => 'Backup status',
    'settings_backup_p1' => 'The backup card shows the last run of the nightly backup: state, schedule, next run, age, sizes, free disk space and the retention. The host creates the backup via cron (script backup.sh); the portal only displays the status and cannot trigger or download a backup.',
    'settings_backup_p2' => 'Schedule and next run come from the cron entry that backup.sh reports on every run, so they are not an estimate. If that report is missing (for example a backup started by hand on a host without a cron entry), the card says so explicitly and derives the expectation from the last run instead.',
    'settings_backup_p3' => 'States: OK (last run succeeded), Failed (check the log), Stale (the expected run did not happen within a two-hour grace window, check cron), Low disk space (under 10 percent free), Unknown (the script has never run or the status mount is missing). When not OK, a banner also appears on the dashboard.',
    'settings_backup_p4' => 'Retention: every run writes two files, the database dump and the configuration archive. For each of them the newest runs are kept (14 by default) and the script deletes older ones right after the backup. On a daily schedule that means you can go back roughly 14 days. The files live on the Docker host under Docker/backups/, not in the portal.',

    'transfer_heading' => 'Export and import a mission',
    'transfer_p1' => 'On a mission detail page you can export it as a JSON file (with VMs, network interfaces, disks and package links). The mission list re-imports such a file and creates a new mission. This is a transfer path between environments, not a backup.',
    'transfer_p2' => 'MAC addresses and MECM ids are never exported or imported; imported VMs start freshly registered. Before importing, a preview shows the counts as well as missing VLANs or already-used VM names.',
    'transfer_p3' => 'Missing VLANs and globally already-used VM names block the import (SCCM device names must be unique); packages that are not found are only skipped and reported. The preview is valid for :minutes minutes.',
    'transfer_p4' => 'The new mission name must be unique and must not contain spaces; if it is already taken, just enter a different one in the preview. The uploaded file may be at most 2 MB in size.',
    'transfer_p5' => 'The "CSV export" button above the lists is unrelated: it only downloads the currently displayed table, for example for reports in Excel. CSV files cannot be imported; the import only accepts the JSON file from a mission export.',

    'ops_heading' => 'Day-to-day operations',
    'ops_p1' => 'A short routine for rotating admin staff. Detailed steps are in the operations docs (docs/operations).',
    'ops_daily' => 'Daily: keep an eye on the dashboard banner and the integrations traffic light.',
    'ops_weekly' => 'Weekly: spot-check the log viewer, review the backup card on the settings page.',
    'ops_monthly' => 'Monthly: run the restore proof (restore_test.sh).',

    'ops_jobs_heading' => 'Where and when the background jobs run',
    'ops_jobs_p1' => 'There is exactly one cron job, and it lives on the Docker host, not in the portal and not in a container. Everything else is a long-running loop inside the stack that needs no schedule:',
    'ops_jobs_backup' => 'Backup (the only cron job): an entry in /etc/cron.d/virtusphere-backup on the Docker host, usually daily at 06:00. It starts scripts/backup.sh, which writes the MySQL dump and a configuration archive to Docker/backups/. The script checks the dump for minimum size and gzip integrity and prunes older runs. Its output goes to the log /var/log/virtusphere-backup.log.',
    'ops_jobs_deploy' => 'Deploy worker: the deploy-worker container, no cron. It asks for due deploy jobs every :seconds seconds and runs them in order. Scheduled jobs wait until their time has come.',
    'ops_jobs_maintenance' => 'Maintenance worker: the maintenance-worker container, no cron. It wakes every :sleep seconds and runs jobs on their own intervals: the MECM reachability probe every :probe minutes, purging of old logs, heartbeat evaluation.',
    'ops_jobs_esxi' => 'ESXi inventory fetch: also the maintenance worker, on the interval configured under Settings (default every :hours hours).',
    'ops_jobs_p2' => 'One command on the host installs or changes the backup schedule; editing the crontab by hand is not needed:',
    'ops_jobs_p3' => 'On every run backup.sh reads that cron file back and reports the schedule it found to the portal. The backup card under Settings therefore shows the real schedule, the next run, and from when a missing run counts as overdue. If a systemd timer runs the backup instead of cron, it reports its own next elapse directly.',

    'ops_restore_heading' => 'Downloading or restoring a backup',
    'ops_restore_p1' => 'Both happen on the Docker host, not in the portal. The backups live there under Docker/backups/ as db-<time>.sql.gz and config-<time>.tar.gz. For each of the two files the newest runs are kept (14 by default) and older ones are deleted automatically, so a daily backup lets you go back roughly 14 days.',
    'ops_restore_p2' => 'The portal deliberately offers neither a download nor a restore. Only the status directory is mounted read-only into the PHP container, never the backups themselves, because the configuration archive contains the .env with the database root password. The restore steps are in docs/operations/backup.md; to check whether a dump is usable, scripts/restore_test.sh loads it into a throwaway container without touching the running stack.',
    'ops_restore_p3' => 'The JSON export of a mission is a transfer path between environments; the CSV export of a list is only a table export for reports. Neither is a backup: the files carry neither users nor credentials, MAC addresses or MECM ids, and cannot restore an environment.',

    'ops_backup_heading' => 'Backup symptoms and actions',
    'ops_backup_failed' => 'State "Failed": check the cron log and the error message on the card; is the stack running, is there free space?',
    'ops_backup_stale' => 'State "Stale": the expected run did not happen, grace window included; check the cron entry and test the script manually once.',
    'ops_backup_disk_low' => 'State "Low disk space": the backup volume is filling up; check retention or disk space.',
    'ops_backup_unknown' => 'State "Unknown" after an update: the status mount is missing or the script never ran; check the mount and start a backup.',
    'ops_backup_more' => 'Details and the full troubleshooting table are in docs/operations/backup.md.',

    'settings_https_heading' => 'Enabling HTTPS',
    'settings_https_p1' => 'On the LAN the portal deliberately starts on HTTP. HTTPS is added later on the "HTTPS" tab, in three steps that build on each other and without restarting the stack: upload the certificate, enable the listener, then enable the redirect. Every step can be reverted on its own.',
    'settings_https_p2' => 'Certificate: either a PFX file from the Windows domain CA (password in the field below) or a PEM pair of certificate and private key. The FQDN of the portal must appear as a subject alternative name (SAN); the CN alone is no longer enough for current browsers. The card then shows issuer, validity and fingerprint, and warns from :days remaining days on. An expired certificate, and a key that does not match the certificate, are rejected. The private key is only stored, never displayed again.',
    'settings_https_p3' => 'Use the toggles in this order. "Enable HTTPS" serves the portal on HTTPS on port :port as well, and the web server picks that up within seconds. Only once the HTTPS address has been checked in a browser, enable the redirect; portal pages then move from HTTP to HTTPS. Enable HSTS last and only if HTTPS is meant to stay, because browsers remember that instruction for :days days, even after it is switched off again.',
    'settings_https_p4' => 'The way back is always open: HTTP stays reachable in any case, and the machine interface (MECM, PowerShell, Ansible) is never redirected. If a browser does not trust the certificate and the portal looks unreachable after the redirect, switch the redirect off again. If the web server rejects a broken configuration, the last valid one stays live. The failure modes, including the emergency exit through the database, are documented in docs/operations/https.md.',

    'settings_time_heading' => 'Time and timezone',
    'settings_time_p1' => 'The timezone only affects how times are displayed in the portal. Internally everything is stored and compared in UTC; changing it only shifts the displayed times. After the initial rollout, existing times therefore appear in local time (Europe/Berlin: plus 1 to 2 hours over UTC).',
    'settings_time_p2' => 'The card shows the current server time with its zone. If the browser clock differs a lot from the server time, a warning appears. The host sets the system clock via NTP, not the portal.',

    'deploy_schedule_heading' => 'Schedule and staggering',
    'deploy_schedule_p1' => 'A job starts immediately or at a set time. Times are in the portal timezone; internally scheduling is in UTC. A scheduled job is only claimed by the worker at its time; if the worker is offline it is caught up afterwards in order.',
    'deploy_schedule_p2' => 'Staggering creates one job per VM that start one after another every N minutes (full, power-cycle and start modes only). In the job list they carry a batch marker and their scheduled time.',
    'deploy_schedule_p3' => 'When a time or a stagger is set, a preview of the computed start times appears before queuing. "Cancel group" stops all still-waiting jobs of a batch; a job that is already running finishes.',

    'deploy_storage_heading' => 'Storage requirements when queuing',
    'deploy_storage_p1' => 'As soon as a mission is selected, the form shows how much disk space the job will use per target datastore. It counts the provisioned sizes of all disks of the checked VMs, so ten machines with 50 GB each add up to 500 GB. VMs with their own datastore get their own row. A VM without its own disks is created with the default disk (50 GB, thick) and counted at that size in the storage estimate.',
    'deploy_storage_p2' => 'Once an ESXi credential is chosen, the table compares the requirement against the free space of the last inventory pull and marks every row as sufficient or as probably too small. Unchecking VMs recalculates the totals immediately. If the credential reports nothing for a datastore, the assessment stays open.',
    'deploy_storage_p3' => 'This is a snapshot and never blocks a job. The numbers are as old as the last pull, and thin disks initially use less than their provisioned size. For a scheduled job the same table appears again in the schedule preview before you confirm.',
    'deploy_storage_p4' => 'One limit the table does not know yet: a datastore in maintenance mode looks like any other, and its free space counts towards the assessment even though nothing can be created there right now. The job then fails visibly on the ESXi host, nothing goes wrong silently.',

    'deploy_warn_heading' => 'Inventory warnings when queuing',
    'deploy_warn_p1' => 'Two separate hints, neither blocks: the mission warning appears when a value of the mission is not in the inventory of any host (value probably stale). The host warning appears live while choosing the ESXi credential, when a value exists on other hosts but not on the selected one (check the host choice). The same value never appears in both hints.',
    'deploy_warn_p2' => 'Mission deploys claim the queue before inventory fetches; a burst of automatic fetches therefore does not delay a deploy.',
    'deploy_warn_p3' => 'Ignoring a host warning does not block the job: it starts, but creating the VMs fails on a host that lacks the named datastore or datacenter. The job then ends as "failed" with the module message in the log. The warnings are deliberately only the early signal; the inventory is a mirror and must never block.',

    'deploy_retry_heading' => 'Running a failed job again',
    'deploy_retry_p1' => 'Only a failed or cancelled job gets the "Run again" button in the job list. A succeeded job is queued through the start form instead, so that the readiness preview runs once more; a running job is cancelled, not retried. The automatic inventory pulls of the system cannot be retried, they schedule themselves.',
    'deploy_retry_p2' => 'Running it again creates a new job with the same content and the same credentials; the old job and its log stay, and the new log points back to it. The new job starts immediately, even when the original one was scheduled or part of a staggered group, and it is attributed to the person who ran it again.',
    'deploy_retry_p3' => 'Before it is queued, the same checks run as for a fresh job: a mission may only have one active job, and mission, VMs and credentials must still exist. If any of them was deleted since the first attempt, running it again fails with a message instead of producing a half-valid job.',

    'bulk_heading' => 'Several VMs at once',
    'bulk_p1' => 'In the VM list you can select several VMs with checkboxes and delete them together or reset their MECM ID. Deleting only removes the portal records, never anything on the hypervisor.',
    'bulk_p2' => 'Individual VMs are skipped and named with a reason in the result, for example when the mission currently has an active deploy job or a VM lacks the imported MAC needed for the MECM-ID reset. At most 200 VMs per action.',

    'hotplug_heading' => 'CPU/RAM hot-add',
    'hotplug_p1' => 'The VM editor lets you enable hot-add for CPU and RAM (on by default). This later allows adding CPU/RAM while the VM runs, but it is only applied when the VM is created on ESXi; existing VMs are not reconfigured. The guest operating system must support hot-add.',

    'creator_heading' => 'Creator of missions and VMs',
    'creator_p1' => 'Missions, templates and VMs record who created them. The name is taken from the signed-in session at creation time and never changes afterwards; editing a VM later does not make you its creator. The field is therefore read-only and cannot be overwritten.',
    'creator_p2' => 'When something is copied, the creator follows whoever did the work:',
    'creator_capture' => 'Saving a mission as a template: the template belongs to the person who saved it. The captured VMs keep their original creator.',
    'creator_clone' => 'Cloning a template into a mission: the new mission and its VMs belong to the person who cloned it, not to the author of the template.',
    'creator_import' => 'Importing a mission from a file: the mission belongs to the importing person. The VMs keep the creator recorded in the file.',
    'creator_unknown' => 'A field showing "Unknown" has no creator on record. This applies to missions and VMs that predate this feature, and to anything created through the old desktop interface, where only a token signs in and no user is known. No name is guessed in that case.',

    'location_heading' => 'Datacenter and datastore',
    'location_p1' => 'Both fields are selection lists fed from the ESXi inventory. For the datastore, each name carries the free space of the last pull. The mission value is the default and is inherited by every VM of the mission when it is deployed. In the VM editor the empty option reads "Inherit from mission" and names the inherited value in brackets. A value chosen there applies to that one VM only.',
    'location_p2' => 'Four things matter here:',
    'location_datacenter_optional' => 'The mission datastore is mandatory, the datacenter is not. If you leave it empty, the deploy uses the datacenter of the ESXi credential you choose there, provided that host reports exactly one. A single ESXi host always does. If the credential reports several (vCenter) or has never been pulled, the portal rejects the job when you queue it and says why.',
    'location_datastore_scope' => 'A different datastore only takes effect when the VM is created. An existing VM is not moved to the other storage by a later change. A different datacenter, by contrast, applies to every action.',
    'location_per_disk' => 'A separate datastore per disk is currently not possible. The Ansible module that creates the VM sets the datastore at VM level and thereby overrides any per-disk setting. All disks of a VM therefore live on the same datastore.',
    'location_single_host' => 'The target host is only chosen when deploying, so the list shows the values of all ESXi credentials. If all of them report the same names, for example the usual ha-datacenter across several single hosts, the list stays a plain enumeration; a single datastore is then preselected in the form and the datacenter field disappears from the mission and the VM editor, because it could not change anything. If the credentials differ, the options are grouped by credential and nothing is preselected; the free space is then shown per credential, otherwise the smallest reported value is used.',
    'location_p3' => 'A stored value the inventory does not know stays selectable as its own option, marked "not in the current inventory", and is never lost. While the inventory of a kind is still empty the field shows a free-text input instead, so nobody is locked out. New values then reach the list through an inventory pull on the integrations page. A deviating value appears there under "Deviations" and never prevents a deploy.',

    'esxi_inv_heading' => 'ESXi inventory',
    'esxi_inv_p1' => 'The portal reads datacenters, datastores, portgroups and host capacity read-only from the registered ESXi credentials at a regular interval. The values are snapshots from the last fetch, not live monitoring. The interval is set under Settings (default :hours hours); a manual refresh and an immediate pull after adding a credential are also available.',
    'esxi_inv_p2' => 'If a fetch fails, an error category appears (unreachable, login, permissions, Ansible host, parsing). On a wrong login the automatic fetch pauses until the credential is changed (lockout protection). The cache is kept on errors; nothing is deleted.',
    'esxi_inv_rights_heading' => 'Required ESXi rights',
    'esxi_inv_rights_read' => 'Inventory (read-only): read access to datacenters, datastores, network/portgroups and host objects.',
    'esxi_inv_rights_deploy' => 'Deploy (playbooks): create VMs, use datastore space, assign networks, power operations, and disk/NIC configuration.',
    'esxi_inv_p3' => 'If a right is missing, the "insufficient permissions" category appears; then extend the account\'s ESXi role. Details are in docs/operations/esxi-inventory.md.',
    'esxi_inv_deviation' => 'Values that are not in the current inventory show up as a deviation (table here, badge on the mission list, hint when deploying). Missions, per-VM overrides and the VLANs of the network interfaces are checked; the badge also lights up when only one VM of the mission is affected. This blocks nothing; it just flags a value to check. If a VLAN was renamed on ESXi, the guided mass reassignment moves all assignments of one VLAN name to an active entry; it affects portal records only, never ESXi. The form only appears when there is an actual VLAN deviation.',
    'esxi_inv_refresh_pause' => 'The "Refresh all" button skips paused credentials (lockout protection) and says so in the notice. A paused credential can still be retried deliberately via its own refresh button, or reactivated by saving the credential.',
    'esxi_inv_ampel_heading' => 'Traffic light of the inventory fetch',
    'esxi_inv_ampel_p1' => 'The traffic light shows the health of the fetch only. Red: login paused or at least :streak consecutive failures. Yellow: last fetch failed, never succeeded, or the last success is older than :factor times the interval. Green: last fetch successful and current. With interval 0 (automation off) the staleness warning is dropped; only real failures colour the light. Host properties such as a free licence or an HA cluster do not colour the light; they appear as their own badges (see "Host notes"). The same traffic light appears on the credentials page and as the "Hypervisor" dashboard tile; they cannot drift apart.',
    'esxi_inv_catalog_heading' => 'VLAN catalog: presence and VLAN ID',
    'esxi_inv_catalog_presence' => 'The "On ESXi" column shows on how many successfully fetched hosts a portgroup exists. Partial presence (say on 3 of 7 hosts) is marked yellow and names the missing hosts: a deploy to one of those hosts would not find the portgroup.',
    'esxi_inv_catalog_vlanid' => 'The "VLAN ID" column compares the IDs of same-named portgroups across hosts. Different IDs are marked red, because the same name with another ID means another network. Trunks/ranges are not compared; older fetches without ID data show a placeholder until the next fetch.',
    'esxi_inv_templates' => 'The deviation report also shows templates (marked "Template"): a stale VLAN living only in a template would otherwise silently propagate into every mission created from it. Clicking a VLAN deviation value prefills the mass reassignment.',

    'esxi_test_heading' => 'Testing a credential',
    'esxi_test_p1' => 'The portal never talks to ESXi directly. For an ESXi account, the "Test" button on the credentials page queues the same inventory pull that saving the credential triggers: over the Ansible host, the exact path a deploy takes. Because that pull is a queued job, the message after the click only says it was queued.',
    'esxi_test_p2' => 'The result then lives on this page as a traffic light, permanently, rather than as a one-off message. The raw output is in the job log. An Ansible account is still tested immediately, because that path runs from the portal itself.',

    'esxi_cap_heading' => 'Reading the host notes',
    'esxi_cap_p1' => 'After a successful pull the portal shows what the host is (product, version, licence, direct or through vCenter) and what it is doing. These notes do not say something is broken; they say what the host can do:',
    'esxi_cap_unknown' => 'When a fact is unknown, no note is shown at all. It is never read as "everything is fine". A job is only refused when the fact comes from a fresh successful pull; stale facts merely warn and let the job run.',

    'esxi_cause_heading' => 'Causes of a failed pull',
    'esxi_cause_p1' => 'When a pull fails, the portal maps the cause to a fixed category. The original technical text sits behind "technical details" on the alert and in the job log.',
    'esxi_cause_th_cause' => 'Category',
    'esxi_cause_th_meaning' => 'Meaning',
    'esxi_cause_th_fix' => 'What to do',
    'esxi_cause_host_placeholder' => 'the host',
    'esxi_cause_fix_dns' => 'Check the hostname in the credential, plus the DNS and search domain of the portal container.',
    'esxi_cause_fix_unreachable' => 'Check network, firewall and port. The pull retries automatically at the next interval; the existing cache is kept.',
    'esxi_cause_fix_tls' => 'Only concerns the SSH connection to the Ansible host. The ESXi pull does not verify certificates.',
    'esxi_cause_fix_auth' => 'Correct the username and secret. The automatic pull pauses until the credential changes, so the ESXi account cannot be locked out.',
    'esxi_cause_fix_authz' => 'Extend the role of the ESXi account. Read rights are enough for the inventory, but not for deploying or autostart.',
    'esxi_cause_fix_http' => 'No longer occurs for ESXi pulls. If the category shows on an old record, a fresh pull clears it.',
    'esxi_cause_fix_ssh' => 'Check the Ansible credential and the Ansible host, exactly as for a failed deploy.',
    'esxi_cause_fix_parse' => 'Look at the job log. Usually the Ansible collection version does not match the host.',
    'esxi_cause_fix_config' => 'Check host, username and type in the credential.',

    'autostart_heading' => 'Autostart on the ESXi host',
    'autostart_p1' => 'The "Start VMs" mode powers the VMs on once. The "Apply ESXi autostart policy" mode instead writes them into the host autostart list for good, so they come up on their own after a reboot or a power loss. It is the same list the host client shows under "Manage, System, Autostart".',
    'autostart_inherit_heading' => 'Inheritance: the mission sets the default, a VM may differ',
    'autostart_inherit_p1' => 'The mission carries the host defaults, each VM optionally its own. Leave a delay field on the VM blank and the mission value applies (default :seconds seconds). Change the mission value later and, on the next autostart run, every VM that set nothing of its own follows.',
    'autostart_inherit_p2' => 'Careful: 0 means "no wait" and is not the same as "inherit". A blank field inherits, a 0 starts immediately.',
    'autostart_limits_heading' => 'Where autostart has no effect',
    'autostart_limit_license' => 'Free ESXi licence: the API is read-only. The job aborts with a clear message instead of appearing to succeed.',
    'autostart_limit_ha' => 'A host inside a vSphere HA cluster: ESXi disables autostart there. The HA restart priority controls the order instead.',
    'autostart_limit_tools' => 'The "shut down the guest" action and the heartbeat option need VMware Tools in the guest. Without them the stop delay elapses and the VM is powered off hard, and the heartbeat option buys no time.',
    'autostart_heartbeat_heading' => 'What the heartbeat option does',
    'autostart_heartbeat_p1' => 'The host starts the VMs one after another. Without the option it waits out the full start delay between two VMs, even when the previous one has long been up. With the option it powers on the next VM as soon as the VMware Tools of the previous one report in; the start delay then only acts as an upper bound in case nothing reports.',
    'autostart_heartbeat_p2' => 'Turn it on when VMware Tools run in every VM of the mission: the boot sequence gets shorter and the order more reliable, because the next VM only starts once the previous one is really there, not merely powered on. Turn it off when a VM without Tools is in the set, or when a service needs post-boot time the Tools do not capture: fixed delays are more honest then.',
    'autostart_limit_order' => 'There is no field for a start number: an open bug in the Ansible collection rejects any number above 1. The order follows from the start delays.',
    'autostart_limit_shared_host' => 'When VMs of several missions live on one host, they share its defaults and the last mission written wins them. That is why the delays can be overridden per VM.',
    'autostart_run_heading' => 'Applying and withdrawing',
    'autostart_run_p1' => 'The mode only writes the VMs selected in the job. A VM whose checkbox was cleared loses its entry on the next run that includes it, not before.',
    'autostart_run_p1b' => 'A VM only participates when its own checkbox is set AND the mission has autostart switched on. While the mission switch is off, the autostart fields in the VM editor are locked; a value already set stays stored and takes effect once the mission is switched on.',
    'autostart_run_p2' => 'The full pipeline appends the autostart step only for a mission that enabled it. The host autostart switch is never turned off, because VMs of other missions may live on it.',
];
